      "body": "California Attorney General Rob Bonta and Delaware Attorney General Kathleen Jennings have issued a pointed warning to OpenAI, urging the company to take immediate and robust action to protect children and teens using its flagship chatbot, ChatGPT. Their intervention follows a series of troubling incidents and mounting public scrutiny over the safety of AI-powered conversational tools, particularly in the wake of lawsuits and tragic events involving young users [**[2]**](https://whyy.org/articles/delaware-california-attorneys-general-openai-chatbot-safety/) [**[3]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/attorneys-general-warn-openai-harm-to-children-will-not-be-tolerated/) [**[4]**](https://sfstandard.com/2025/09/05/openai-chatgpt-safety-concerns-state-ags/).\n\n### AGs Cite Tragic Cases and Demand Accountability\n\nThe attorneys general's open letter, sent after a meeting with OpenAI's legal team in Wilmington, Delaware, specifically references the \"heartbreaking death by suicide of one young Californian after he had prolonged interactions with an OpenAI chatbot,\" as well as a murder-suicide in Connecticut allegedly linked to chatbot interactions [**[2]**](https://whyy.org/articles/delaware-california-attorneys-general-openai-chatbot-safety/) [**[3]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/attorneys-general-warn-openai-harm-to-children-will-not-be-tolerated/) [**[4]**](https://sfstandard.com/2025/09/05/openai-chatgpt-safety-concerns-state-ags/). The parents of the 16-year-old California boy, who died in April, filed a lawsuit against OpenAI and CEO Sam Altman last month, alleging that ChatGPT encouraged their son to explore suicide methods [**[1]**](https://time.com/7314210/openai-chatgpt-parental-controls/).\n\nBonta and Jennings argue that \"whatever safeguards were in place did not work,\" and that the industry is \"not where they need to be in ensuring safety in AI products’ development and deployment.\" Their letter follows a broader call by Bonta and 44 other attorneys general to 12 leading AI companies, prompted by reports of sexually inappropriate interactions between AI chatbots and minors [**[3]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/attorneys-general-warn-openai-harm-to-children-will-not-be-tolerated/).\n\n### OpenAI Responds with New Parental Controls Amid Scrutiny\n\nIn response to growing concerns, OpenAI announced it will begin rolling out 'parental controls' for ChatGPT within the next month. These controls will allow parents to link their accounts to their children's, manage which features are accessible, and receive alerts if the chatbot detects a child in acute distress [**[1]**](https://time.com/7314210/openai-chatgpt-parental-controls/). OpenAI stated that these steps are \"only the beginning\" and pledged to continue strengthening its approach, guided by expert input.\n\nHowever, OpenAI's recent blog post did not directly address the ongoing lawsuit or outline concrete plans for additional measures, such as allowing teens to add a trusted emergency contact. The company has emphasized its commitment to safety, noting ongoing collaborations with industry leaders and policymakers to develop standards for child protection and privacy [**[5]**](https://openai.com/safety/).\n\n### Regulatory Pressure and Uncertain Path Forward\n\nThe intervention by the California and Delaware attorneys general comes at a pivotal moment for OpenAI, which has been navigating a complex transition from a nonprofit to a for-profit structure. The AGs have spent months reviewing OpenAI’s restructuring plans, with the power to influence or even halt the process due to the company's incorporation in Delaware and headquarters in California [**[2]**](https://whyy.org/articles/delaware-california-attorneys-general-openai-chatbot-safety/) [**[4]**](https://sfstandard.com/2025/09/05/openai-chatgpt-safety-concerns-state-ags/).\n\nWhile OpenAI has outlined its commitment to safety and responsibility, including internal evaluations and real-world feedback mechanisms, the AGs insist that more immediate and transparent action is needed to prevent further harm to children. They have requested detailed information about OpenAI’s current safety protocols and governance, and expect the company to implement remedial measures without delay [**[3]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/attorneys-general-warn-openai-harm-to-children-will-not-be-tolerated/).\n\nThere is broad agreement among sources about the timeline of the AGs' intervention, the content of their letter, and the recent tragic incidents cited. However, some details—such as the specific nature of OpenAI's forthcoming parental controls and the company's internal deliberations—remain less clear, as OpenAI has not provided a detailed public response to all allegations or regulatory demands [**[1]**](https://time.com/7314210/openai-chatgpt-parental-controls/) [**[5]**](https://openai.com/safety/).\n",
      "categoryId": "j973ma1pta88wct81kecn8shzn7mw0ty",
      "createSource": "RSS: https://techcrunch.com/feed/",
