      "body": "The personalized AI companion app Dot, launched in 2024 by the startup New Computer, will officially shut down on October 5, 2025. The decision, announced by co-founders Sam Whitmore and former Apple designer Jason Yuan, comes at a time of heightened scrutiny over the psychological and ethical risks posed by AI-driven emotional support tools [**[2]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/personalized-ai-companion-app-dot-is-shutting-down/), [**[1]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-companion-app-dot-shutting-safety-concerns-2509/).\n\n### Founders Cite Diverging Visions as Primary Reason\n\nAccording to the official statement from New Computer, the primary reason for Dot’s closure is a divergence in the founders’ visions for the product’s future [**[2]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/personalized-ai-companion-app-dot-is-shutting-down/), [**[5]**](https://futurism.com/ai-dot-companion-controversy). Whitmore and Yuan explained that after a year of development and user feedback, their goals for expanding Dot’s capabilities began to diverge. Rather than compromise, they chose to wind down operations. The app will remain operational until October 5, 2025, allowing users to export their chat logs and data before the shutdown [**[4]**](https://www.techshotsapp.com/artificial-intelligence/ai-companion-app-dot-shuts-down-citing-diverging-vision).\n\nWhile the founders did not directly address the mounting controversy over AI mental health risks in their public statement, they acknowledged the emotional impact the shutdown may have on users who viewed Dot as a friend and confidante [**[5]**](https://futurism.com/ai-dot-companion-controversy).\n\n### Safety Concerns and the \"AI Psychosis\" Debate\n\nDot’s closure comes amid a wave of scrutiny directed at AI chatbots and companion apps. Reports have emerged of users developing unhealthy dependencies or delusional thinking after prolonged interactions with AI companions, a phenomenon some experts have dubbed \"AI psychosis\" [**[1]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-companion-app-dot-shutting-safety-concerns-2509/), [**[2]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/personalized-ai-companion-app-dot-is-shutting-down/), [**[3]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-shuts-dot-leaves-big-questions-digital-souls-2509/). Clinical psychologist Derrick Hull, cited in recent coverage, suggests these cases are more accurately described as AI-induced delusions, as symptoms often dissipate when challenged by another AI [**[1]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-companion-app-dot-shutting-safety-concerns-2509/).\n\nThe shutdown also coincides with broader legal and regulatory actions against major AI companies. For example, OpenAI is currently facing lawsuits and government inquiries after reports that its chatbot may have contributed to a teenager’s suicide [**[2]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/personalized-ai-companion-app-dot-is-shutting-down/), [**[3]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-shuts-dot-leaves-big-questions-digital-souls-2509/). Two U.S. attorneys general recently sent a letter to OpenAI expressing concern over the potential harm to vulnerable users.\n\n### Market Realities and the Future of AI Companions\n\nDespite Dot’s ambitions, the app struggled to achieve broad market traction. While the company claimed \"hundreds of thousands\" of users, independent data suggests only about 24,500 iOS downloads [**[3]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-shuts-dot-leaves-big-questions-digital-souls-2509/). The shutdown highlights the challenges faced by smaller startups in the AI companion space, especially as ethical and safety concerns mount.\n\nIndustry observers note that Dot’s closure is unusual in a market where many virtual companion apps continue to thrive. However, the event has intensified calls for clearer ethical guidelines and greater transparency in the development of AI systems designed for emotionally sensitive contexts [**[3]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-shuts-dot-leaves-big-questions-digital-souls-2509/), [**[4]**](https://www.techshotsapp.com/artificial-intelligence/ai-companion-app-dot-shuts-down-citing-diverging-vision).\n\nNo major discrepancies exist among the sources regarding the shutdown date, the founders’ stated reasons, or the broader context of AI safety concerns. However, there is some uncertainty regarding the true scale of Dot’s user base, as company claims and independent analytics differ significantly [**[3]**](https://www.ainvest.com/news/ai-shuts-dot-leaves-big-questions-digital-souls-2509/).\n",
      "categoryId": "j97fcsm1n8x73r4pf9jbgmstp17q474r",
      "createSource": "RSS: https://techcrunch.com/feed/",
