      "body": "Tesla’s board of directors is under fire for proposing a $1 trillion pay package for CEO Elon Musk, despite the company scaling back on several of Musk’s most ambitious promises. The compensation plan, which would be the largest in corporate history if approved, comes as Tesla’s production and robotics targets have been significantly reduced compared to earlier projections [**[1]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/06/musks-1t-pay-package-is-full-of-watered-down-versions-of-his-own-broken-promises/), [**[2]**](https://www.wsj.com/business/tesla-musk-pay-package-scaled-back-promises-2025-09-06), [**[3]**](https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/tesla-board-musk-pay-package-controversy-2025-09-06).\n\n### Musk’s Original Ambitions vs. Current Board Proposals\n\nElon Musk previously claimed that Tesla would produce 20 million vehicles and one million humanoid robots annually, setting bold targets for the company’s future [**[1]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/06/musks-1t-pay-package-is-full-of-watered-down-versions-of-his-own-broken-promises/). However, the latest board proposal ties Musk’s compensation to far more modest goals, including lower vehicle production and limited deployment of robotics. Critics argue that the board’s targets are now more achievable but fall short of Musk’s earlier vision.\n\nIndustry analysts note that the revised targets reflect ongoing challenges in scaling up production and commercializing advanced robotics. Tesla’s actual vehicle output in 2024 was approximately 2.3 million units, well below the 20 million figure Musk once cited [**[2]**](https://www.wsj.com/business/tesla-musk-pay-package-scaled-back-promises-2025-09-06), [**[3]**](https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/tesla-board-musk-pay-package-controversy-2025-09-06).\n\n### Shareholder and Public Reaction to the Compensation Plan\n\nThe proposed $1 trillion pay package has sparked outrage among some shareholders and governance experts, who question whether such a large reward is justified given the company’s revised ambitions. Critics argue that the plan rewards Musk for achieving targets that are less ambitious than those he previously set [**[1]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/06/musks-1t-pay-package-is-full-of-watered-down-versions-of-his-own-broken-promises/), [**[2]**](https://www.wsj.com/business/tesla-musk-pay-package-scaled-back-promises-2025-09-06).\n\nTesla’s board defends the package as necessary to retain Musk and incentivize continued innovation. However, some institutional investors have signaled opposition, citing concerns about governance and the alignment of executive compensation with long-term shareholder value [**[3]**](https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/tesla-board-musk-pay-package-controversy-2025-09-06).\n\n### Broader Implications for Corporate Governance and Executive Pay\n\nThe controversy over Musk’s pay package highlights broader debates about executive compensation and accountability in the tech sector. Some experts warn that tying massive rewards to scaled-back targets could set a precedent for other companies, potentially undermining efforts to link pay to genuine performance [**[2]**](https://www.wsj.com/business/tesla-musk-pay-package-scaled-back-promises-2025-09-06).\n\nWhile Tesla’s board maintains that the compensation plan is in the best interest of the company, the outcome of the upcoming shareholder vote remains uncertain. The debate is likely to influence future discussions about executive pay and corporate governance across the industry.\n",
