      "body": "Microsoft has confirmed that its Azure cloud platform experienced disruptions after multiple undersea cables were cut in the Red Sea, affecting connectivity for customers in Europe, Asia, and Africa. The incident, which occurred in early September 2025, has raised concerns about the vulnerability of global internet infrastructure and the potential for geopolitical tensions [**[1]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/07/microsoft-says-azure-affected-after-cables-cut-in-the-red-sea/), [**[2]**](https://theverge.com/2025/09/07/microsoft-azure-red-sea-cable-cut), [**[3]**](https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2025/09/microsoft-azure-red-sea-cable-disruption/), [**[4]**](https://wired.com/story/microsoft-azure-red-sea-cable-cut/), [**[5]**](https://bbc.com/news/technology-azure-red-sea-cable-cut).\n\n### Details of the Cable Disruption and Azure Impact\n\nAccording to Microsoft, the cable cuts occurred on September 5, 2025, and affected several major undersea lines that carry internet traffic between continents. Azure customers reported latency issues, intermittent outages, and degraded performance, particularly in regions dependent on the affected routes [**[1]**](https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/07/microsoft-says-azure-affected-after-cables-cut-in-the-red-sea/), [**[2]**](https://theverge.com/2025/09/07/microsoft-azure-red-sea-cable-cut). Microsoft stated that its engineering teams quickly rerouted traffic and worked with telecom partners to restore service, but some disruptions persisted for several hours.\n\nThe company has not disclosed the full extent of the impact, but sources indicate that other cloud providers may have experienced similar issues. The incident highlights the critical role of undersea cables in supporting global cloud infrastructure and the challenges of maintaining resilience in the face of physical damage.\n\n### Investigation and Unanswered Questions\n\nIt remains unclear who was responsible for cutting the cables or what motivated the incident. Authorities in the region have launched investigations, but as of September 7, 2025, no group has claimed responsibility and no official explanation has been provided [**[3]**](https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2025/09/microsoft-azure-red-sea-cable-disruption/), [**[4]**](https://wired.com/story/microsoft-azure-red-sea-cable-cut/). Some experts speculate that the cuts could be accidental, resulting from shipping or fishing activity, while others warn of possible sabotage linked to regional conflicts.\n\nMicrosoft has urged customers to remain vigilant and assured them that additional safeguards are being implemented to prevent future disruptions. The company is also collaborating with international agencies to monitor the situation and assess long-term risks to cloud infrastructure.\n\n### Broader Implications for Internet Security\n\nThe Red Sea cable incident has reignited debate about the fragility of global internet infrastructure and the need for greater investment in redundancy and security [**[5]**](https://bbc.com/news/technology-azure-red-sea-cable-cut), [**[2]**](https://theverge.com/2025/09/07/microsoft-azure-red-sea-cable-cut). Industry analysts note that undersea cables are often overlooked despite carrying more than 95% of international data. The event has prompted calls for governments and tech companies to collaborate on protecting critical infrastructure from both accidental damage and deliberate attacks.\n\nWhile service has largely been restored, the incident serves as a reminder of the interconnected nature of digital services and the ongoing challenges in securing the backbone of the global internet.\n\n",
